@*
    One civic or origin, said in full.

    A card rather than a tile, because this is a page to read rather than a picker to choose from:
    the name and the icon say which one it is, and everything under them is the answer to a question
    somebody came here with. What it does, who may have it, and what it costs in packs.

    Pressing it picks it out and puts it in the address, so the page can be sent to somebody as it
    stands. The card is not a button though - it holds chips that are, and a button inside a button
    is markup no browser agrees about. The press is on the article and the chips stop it.
*@

<article class="sem-wiki-card @(Row.Playable ? null : "unreachable") @(Chosen ? "chosen" : null)"
         data-entry="@Row.Key" @onclick="OnChoose">
    @*
        The scene the empire wakes up on, above the words - which is where the designer's own picker
        puts it, and for the reason stated there: it is what the option looks like, and reading about
        it before seeing it loses the thing being read about.

        Origins only. Every one of the game's civics has an icon and none has a picture, so the card
        simply does without rather than reserving a space that would be blank three hundred times.
    *@
    @if (Row.Picture is { Length: > 0 } picture)
    {
        <img class="sem-detail-picture" src="@Session.Data.AssetUrl(picture)" alt="" loading="lazy">
    }

    <header>
        @* Only where this shelf has pictures at all - see the same gate on the table. *@
        @if (Icons)
        {
            <GameIcon Path="@Row.Icon" Alt="@Row.Name" />
        }

        <div class="title">
            <h2>@Row.Name</h2>

            <div class="sem-wiki-badges">
                @*
                    Only the ones that cannot be read off the packs beside them. A civic no player
                    can ever take says so; a civic behind a pack says so through the chip, which
                    carries the direction of the gate as well as the name.
                *@
                @if (Row.ClosedShort is { } closed)
                {
                    <span class="sem-badge closed" title="@Row.ClosedWhy">@closed</span>
                }

                <PackChips Packs="@Row.Packs" />
            </div>
        </div>

        @*
            The link to this one entry, in the corner.

            Inside the header rather than over the card, so the scene on an origin pushes it down by
            itself: the art runs edge to edge and a button on top of it would sit on somebody's face.
        *@
        <WikiLinkButton Link="@Link" Name="@Row.Name" />
    </header>

    @if (Row.Tiers.Count == 0)
    {
        @if (Row.Description is { Length: > 0 })
        {
            <p class="sem-wiki-prose">@((MarkupString)Wording.Html(Row.DescriptionKey, string.Empty))</p>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        @*
            An upgrade path, a block per step.

            What every step agrees about stays where it was - the header above, the facts and the
            packs below - so a path says its class and its rarity once. What changes is here: the
            step's own name where the game gave it one, its prose where it differs from the step
            before, what it does, and the tier it is.
        *@
        @foreach (var step in Row.Tiers)
        {
            @* Each step answers to its own key, so a link naming a later tier can be scrolled to. *@
            <div class="sem-wiki-step" data-entry="@step.Key">
                @{ var tier = step.Fact("Tier")?.Text; }

                @if (Heads(step))
                {
                    <div class="heading">
                        <GameIcon Path="@step.Icon" Alt="@step.Name" />
                        <span class="title">@step.Name</span>

                        @if (tier is { Length: > 0 })
                        {
                            <span class="tier">@tier</span>
                        }
                    </div>
                }

                @if (step.Description is { Length: > 0 } && Told.Add(step.Description))
                {
                    <p class="sem-wiki-prose">
                        @((MarkupString)Wording.Html(step.DescriptionKey, string.Empty))
                    </p>
                }

                <EffectsList Effects="@step.Effects" />
            </div>
        }
    }

    @*
        What is true of this kind and not of the others, above what it does. An ethic's cost decides
        whether a reader can afford it at all, which is a question they ask before what it grants.
    *@
    @if (Shared.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="sem-wiki-facts">
            <WikiFacts Facts="@Shared" />
        </div>
    }

    @if (Row.Tiers.Count == 0)
    {
        <EffectsList Effects="@Row.Effects" />
    }

    @if (Row.Gallery.Count > 0)
    {
        <WikiGallery Faces="@Row.Gallery" />
    }

    @*
        Whatever conditions this kind of entry states, each under its own heading. A civic keeps two
        apart - what makes it appear at all against what then makes it legal - an authority states
        only the second, and an ethic states none and so draws none of this.
    *@
    <dl class="sem-wiki-conditions">
        @foreach (var condition in Row.Conditions)
        {
            <div class="@(condition.Stated ? "stated" : null)">
                <dt>@condition.Heading</dt>

                <dd>
                    @if (condition.Outline is { } outline)
                    {
                        <ConditionList Node="@outline" />
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        @condition.Otherwise
                    }
                </dd>
            </div>
        }
    </dl>
</article>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The text this shelf is written in, where a page carries its own.</summary>
    [CascadingParameter(Name = "WikiText")]
    public Localizer? Reader { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whichever reader answers for this shelf.</summary>
    private Localizer Wording => Reader ?? Session.Localizer;

    /// <summary>
    /// The facts that belong to the whole entry rather than to one step of it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Everything, for an entry that is one thing. For an upgrade path, everything but the tier -
    /// which is the one fact that is always about a single step, and is drawn beside that step's
    /// own name instead.
    /// </remarks>
    private IReadOnlyList<WikiFact> Shared =>
        Row.Tiers.Count == 0
            ? Row.Facts
            : [.. Row.Facts.Where(f => !string.Equals(f.Heading, "Tier", StringComparison.Ordinal))];

    /// <summary>
    /// The prose already printed on this card, so a path does not say the same thing three times.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The game writes a second tier's description as a reference to the first's, so the steps of a
    /// path very often read identically. Rebuilt on every render because the card is drawn afresh
    /// each time and a set kept between renders would suppress everything after the first.
    /// </remarks>
    private HashSet<string> Told { get; } = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    protected override void OnParametersSet() => Told.Clear();

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a step of an upgrade path draws a heading of its own.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Every step but the base of the path. The card's header is already that step's name and its
    /// picture, and a numeral saying "1" under both was the card introducing itself three times
    /// before saying anything. Later steps keep all three, because there the name, the picture and
    /// the numeral are the whole answer to which step this is. One chain - Strand Afflicted - has no
    /// tier on its base at all, so position answers for it where the number cannot.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="step">The step.</param>
    /// <returns>True where it draws a name, an icon and a numeral.</returns>
    private bool Heads(WikiRow step) =>
        step.Fact("Tier")?.Text is { Length: > 0 } tier
            ? !string.Equals(tier, "1", StringComparison.Ordinal)
            : !string.Equals(step.Key, Row.Tiers[0].Key, StringComparison.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>The entry to draw.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required WikiRow Row { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether this is the one the address names.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Chosen { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether this shelf has icons, and so whether to keep room for one in the header.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Icons { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Raised when the reader presses it, so the page can put it in the address.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnChoose { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// This entry's own route, which the page knows and the card does not.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Handed down rather than worked out here, because the card knows which entry it is and not
    /// which shelf it is on - the page is the only thing that knows both.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required string Link { get; set; }
}
